Melumzi,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on wind farms, reserves and remote sites; we don't sell packages.

You look after the eBOP at Oyster Bay and site management at Tsitsikamma Community Wind Farm, so you'll know the real question on a wind farm is resilience: when the fibre is cut or stolen, does SCADA stay up, can the O&M crews still work across the site, and are the perimeter cameras still reporting? A standby wireless path that takes over automatically costs far less than one day of lost visibility. We have done this kind of work at Oya Wind Farm and at Eskom Medupi.

I've done a desktop terrain study of both sites from satellite elevation data and the mapped turbine positions; it's attached. One standby backbone with two high sites has line of sight to 68 of the 72 turbines across Oyster Bay and Tsitsikamma, and to both substations. The site positions are desktop candidates until we walk them.

The next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you have, what fails, what it costs you when the fibre goes, and what an owned standby path would look like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours either way. If you can tell me how often the fibre has been cut in the last two years and how long SCADA was blind each time, I'll size the standby path to match.

Can I call you for 10 minutes this week?

